WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.451 --> 00:00:02.611
- Everyone who really shies
away from a challenge.

00:00:02.611 --> 00:00:05.018
I usually kind of jump
at the opportunity to do

00:00:05.018 --> 00:00:06.552
something different and unexpected.

00:00:06.552 --> 00:00:08.211
I've been in the negative
road and I've been in

00:00:08.211 --> 00:00:09.706
the positive road and I would never give

00:00:09.706 --> 00:00:10.981
up the positive road.

00:00:10.981 --> 00:00:13.542
It just gives me, between
my physical fitness

00:00:13.542 --> 00:00:16.672
and my faith I have so
much discipline in my life.

00:00:16.672 --> 00:00:18.083
When I said I wanted to be an actor,

00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:19.598
people kind of chuckled
and then when I said

00:00:19.598 --> 00:00:22.599
I wanted to be a serious
actor, people chuckled.

00:00:22.599 --> 00:00:25.646
- He's an American actor,
producer, business man,

00:00:25.646 --> 00:00:27.581
rapper, and former model.

00:00:27.581 --> 00:00:29.444
He's the co-owner of the
Wahlbergers restaurant

00:00:29.444 --> 00:00:32.748
chain and co-stars in the
reality TV show about it.

00:00:32.748 --> 00:00:34.607
He received a star on the
Hollywood Walk of Fame

00:00:34.607 --> 00:00:36.790
on July 29, 2010.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:38.995
He's Mark Wahlberg and
here's my take on his

00:00:38.995 --> 00:00:40.899
top ten rules for success.

00:00:40.899 --> 00:00:42.699
Rule number one is my
personal favorite and make

00:00:42.699 --> 00:00:44.291
sure to stick around all
the way to the end for

00:00:44.291 --> 00:00:45.886
some special bonus clips.

00:00:45.886 --> 00:00:47.665
Also, as Mark is talking,
if he says something that

00:00:47.665 --> 00:00:49.685
really inspires you,
make sure to copy it down

00:00:49.685 --> 00:00:51.983
to the comments below
and put quotes around it

00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:55.316
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:57.422 --> 00:00:59.376
- I mean, as terrible as
it is, the kid who had

00:00:59.376 --> 00:01:01.943
the girls in the neighborhood,
who had the nicest

00:01:01.943 --> 00:01:03.768
car, sold the most drugs.

00:01:03.768 --> 00:01:06.811
You know, there wasn't
really anybody that I could

00:01:06.811 --> 00:01:09.271
look up to that was from
my situation and really

00:01:09.271 --> 00:01:10.882
made something of himself.

00:01:10.882 --> 00:01:12.810
That's why I feel it's
so important to stay true

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.709
to who I am and where I'm from.

00:01:14.709 --> 00:01:17.550
Because kids like myself do look up to me.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:21.825
I came through it the right
way, so it really gives

00:01:21.825 --> 00:01:25.268
them something to look
at, something to work for.

00:01:25.268 --> 00:01:28.292
That's why I've accomplished
really everything

00:01:28.292 --> 00:01:31.633
that I've set out to
do, but to just stop and

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:34.844
not stay on that mission, I
think would be wrong, too.

00:01:34.844 --> 00:01:37.196
Because things are still getting worse,

00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:38.251
you know what I'm saying.

00:01:38.251 --> 00:01:40.839
Playing a professor in
college, it was a bit of

00:01:40.839 --> 00:01:44.048
a stretch, but something
that I was excited about.

00:01:44.048 --> 00:01:46.680
Everyone who really shies
away from a challenge, I

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.137
jump at the opportunity
to do something different

00:01:49.137 --> 00:01:51.510
and unexpected, but I'm
also the kind of guy

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.669
who's going to make
sure that I'm prepared,

00:01:54.669 --> 00:01:57.489
really cover all the
bases and I don't want to

00:01:57.489 --> 00:02:01.606
look foolish and I certainly
don't want to disappoint.

00:02:01.606 --> 00:02:04.368
- Back growing up, tough time.

00:02:04.368 --> 00:02:06.285
- Yeah, yeah. Somewhat.

00:02:07.588 --> 00:02:10.779
- I mean, could that
kid look at you today.

00:02:10.779 --> 00:02:13.345
Could you ever imagine that
it would turn out this way?

00:02:13.345 --> 00:02:15.391
- You know, somebody asked
me last night, they said,

00:02:15.391 --> 00:02:17.706
"What if Marky Mark met
Mark Wahlberg right now?"

00:02:17.706 --> 00:02:20.055
And I said, well if I
didn't get to say the first

00:02:20.055 --> 00:02:22.320
word he'd probably punch me in the face.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.029
But, you know, if I got
to talk to him, I think

00:02:25.029 --> 00:02:29.248
he would understand and
maybe change his ways.

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:31.391
- [Interviewer] Marky Mark was whom, then?

00:02:31.391 --> 00:02:35.277
- Marky Mark was the
trumped up version of who I

00:02:35.277 --> 00:02:38.539
was in the street when
I was younger and wild

00:02:38.539 --> 00:02:40.420
and just like everybody
else, just trying--

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:41.619
- [Interviewer] Selling drugs.

00:02:41.619 --> 00:02:43.823
- Trying to be the toughest
guy in the neighborhood,

00:02:43.823 --> 00:02:46.517
trying to impress the
girls by stealing a car

00:02:46.517 --> 00:02:49.200
or something, just really foolish stuff.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.099
But that was really all
that I was accustomed to

00:02:53.099 --> 00:02:54.984
and that's all that I could see.

00:02:54.984 --> 00:02:56.486
- You learned it on the street?

00:02:56.486 --> 00:02:58.876
- Yeah.
- How'd you pull out of that?

00:02:58.876 --> 00:03:02.626
- Well, I got incarcerated,
like most people.

00:03:04.124 --> 00:03:06.804
And I think I was the only
one who didn't like it.

00:03:06.804 --> 00:03:09.380
And I couldn't figure that out.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.613
Everybody was just so
content with, whether it

00:03:11.613 --> 00:03:13.395
would be the winter,
'cause it was cold outside,

00:03:13.395 --> 00:03:15.531
and they'd get their free
meals and everything else,

00:03:15.531 --> 00:03:16.948
I just knew everybody and
it just looked like one

00:03:16.948 --> 00:03:18.839
of the corners in my neighborhood.

00:03:18.839 --> 00:03:21.291
And I said, wow, if I
want anything else out of

00:03:21.291 --> 00:03:23.614
this life, I've got to start now.

00:03:23.614 --> 00:03:25.124
- [Interviewer] So, what'd you do?

00:03:25.124 --> 00:03:26.644
- I just prayed.

00:03:26.644 --> 00:03:30.526
Started focusing on my
family and looking at all

00:03:30.526 --> 00:03:33.164
the stuff that I'd put
my mom through, being the

00:03:33.164 --> 00:03:34.371
youngest of nine.

00:03:34.371 --> 00:03:36.822
She wasn't really around enough.

00:03:36.822 --> 00:03:38.611
She was always working
two jobs, my dad worked

00:03:38.611 --> 00:03:41.145
two jobs, so I was kind
of, you know, free to

00:03:41.145 --> 00:03:43.560
run the streets and roam
the streets, but she never

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.339
knew it and of course,
she would never think that

00:03:45.339 --> 00:03:47.453
her baby, her little boy
would do anything wrong.

00:03:47.453 --> 00:03:51.036
So, I just looked at
all those things and I

00:03:51.921 --> 00:03:53.875
realized that I couldn't blame it on my

00:03:53.875 --> 00:03:55.290
neighborhood anymore.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:57.378
I know right from wrong
in the back of my mind.

00:03:57.378 --> 00:04:01.065
I need to do what's right
and if not, just get

00:04:01.065 --> 00:04:03.410
used to the environment that I'm in.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:06.507
You have two choices in life, basically.

00:04:06.507 --> 00:04:09.785
It's basically to succeed or to fail,

00:04:09.785 --> 00:04:11.217
you know what I'm saying.

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:13.421
And I've been in the
negative road and I've been

00:04:13.421 --> 00:04:15.292
in the positive road and
I would never give up

00:04:15.292 --> 00:04:17.102
the positive road, never.

00:04:17.102 --> 00:04:18.215
I would never.

00:04:18.215 --> 00:04:19.876
Even, you know what I'm
saying, even if I was

00:04:19.876 --> 00:04:21.274
the biggest flop in the world.

00:04:21.274 --> 00:04:23.910
- How different do you
think your career, possibly

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:26.606
your life would've been
had you not embraced the

00:04:26.606 --> 00:04:29.722
physical fitness part early on?

00:04:29.722 --> 00:04:30.599
- Oh man.

00:04:30.599 --> 00:04:33.854
It just gives me, between
my physical fitness and

00:04:33.854 --> 00:04:36.476
my faith, I have so much
discipline in my life.

00:04:36.476 --> 00:04:39.926
I have such a positive
outlook on life and the

00:04:39.926 --> 00:04:42.174
opportunities that I
have and the things that

00:04:42.174 --> 00:04:44.218
I've been able to accomplish in my life.

00:04:44.218 --> 00:04:46.404
It starts out, you know
I'm up at 4:30 in the

00:04:46.404 --> 00:04:48.473
morning, I'm in my
kitchen having breakfast,

00:04:48.473 --> 00:04:50.985
five o'clock I'm in the
gym, 6:30 I've already had

00:04:50.985 --> 00:04:53.093
another shake and 7:30 I'm eating a couple

00:04:53.093 --> 00:04:55.654
turkey burgers, dropping
the kids off at school

00:04:55.654 --> 00:04:58.057
and by nine o'clock I'm
having another meal and

00:04:58.057 --> 00:04:59.987
I've already been to
church and worked out and

00:04:59.987 --> 00:05:02.617
I feel like there isn't
anything I can't accomplish.

00:05:02.617 --> 00:05:04.833
- Was The Departed one
for them or one for you?

00:05:04.833 --> 00:05:07.172
- Departed was one for
me, and one for Marty.

00:05:07.172 --> 00:05:08.638
You know, when you get
a chance to work with

00:05:08.638 --> 00:05:10.608
Marty Scorsese, you got to show up.

00:05:10.608 --> 00:05:12.174
- And you get an Oscar nom--

00:05:12.174 --> 00:05:14.745
- But they didn't pay
me at all, but you know.

00:05:14.745 --> 00:05:16.110
- Did you do it for scale?

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.962
- It was pretty cheap.

00:05:17.962 --> 00:05:19.881
I don't know if it was scale,
but it was pretty cheap.

00:05:19.881 --> 00:05:21.321
But you know, but hey.

00:05:21.321 --> 00:05:22.797
- I guess 10 million
for Planet of the Apes,

00:05:22.797 --> 00:05:24.005
you go, I can do one for Marty.

00:05:24.005 --> 00:05:25.245
- Exactly, exactly.

00:05:25.245 --> 00:05:26.513
Well, then you got to
do 10 for free to make

00:05:26.513 --> 00:05:27.927
up for that one.

00:05:27.927 --> 00:05:29.437
(audience laughing)

00:05:29.437 --> 00:05:31.822
But ah, you know you get
an opportunity to work

00:05:31.822 --> 00:05:33.810
with Marty Scorsese, I
don't mind cutting a check

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.271
to have that opportunity.

00:05:35.271 --> 00:05:37.390
A way to prove yourself
is to better yourself.

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:39.436
- [Announcer] In a coup for
Optus, he's setting up their

00:05:39.436 --> 00:05:41.121
small business campaign.

00:05:41.121 --> 00:05:42.917
- When I said I wanted to be an actor,

00:05:42.917 --> 00:05:44.360
they laughed at me.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:45.942
When I said I wanted to be a producer,

00:05:45.942 --> 00:05:47.604
they told me to stay in my lane.

00:05:47.604 --> 00:05:49.472
When I said I wanted to
open up a restaurant,

00:05:49.472 --> 00:05:52.254
they said it would never
happen, but it did happen.

00:05:52.254 --> 00:05:54.524
- Mark Wahlberg, thanks for joining us.

00:05:54.524 --> 00:05:56.346
In the ad, you say that
when you told people

00:05:56.346 --> 00:05:57.910
back in Massachusetts
that you wanted to be an

00:05:57.910 --> 00:05:59.551
actor, that you were laughed at.

00:05:59.551 --> 00:06:00.780
Why did they laugh at you?

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.709
- Well, first I was beaten up,

00:06:02.709 --> 00:06:05.069
and then I was laughed at.

00:06:05.069 --> 00:06:06.721
It's one of those things,
it was just never really

00:06:06.721 --> 00:06:08.918
cool to want to be in
the arts, you know, you

00:06:08.918 --> 00:06:10.623
were either going to be
a police officer or a

00:06:10.623 --> 00:06:13.731
crook, or if you were
lucky enough to get out

00:06:13.731 --> 00:06:16.175
as an athlete, or if
you were smart enough to

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:18.578
get to go to one of the great educational

00:06:18.578 --> 00:06:21.054
institutes in and around the Boston area.

00:06:21.054 --> 00:06:23.317
My brothers has a lot
of success and stuff and

00:06:23.317 --> 00:06:26.303
I have an album coming
out and I've worked hard

00:06:26.303 --> 00:06:28.196
for it though, you know what I'm saying?

00:06:28.196 --> 00:06:29.724
A lot of people will
think, oh his brother's

00:06:29.724 --> 00:06:31.321
rich and this and that, you know.

00:06:31.321 --> 00:06:33.503
He's just, you know what I'm saying,

00:06:33.503 --> 00:06:34.890
everything's been handed to him.

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:36.397
A lot of people say that to me now.

00:06:36.397 --> 00:06:38.515
I've worked hard for it though,
you know what I'm saying.

00:06:38.515 --> 00:06:40.160
- Is there a type of genre
of film that maybe you

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:42.406
haven't done that you want to explore?

00:06:42.406 --> 00:06:43.947
- If there is, I'm going to do it.

00:06:43.947 --> 00:06:45.332
I'm going to take a crack at it.

00:06:45.332 --> 00:06:47.291
I always wanted to be able
to do it all, you know.

00:06:47.291 --> 00:06:49.212
I waited for the right
time to kind of creep

00:06:49.212 --> 00:06:53.642
into the comedy thing and
it's been good so far,

00:06:53.642 --> 00:06:56.365
I want to be able to do it all.

00:06:56.365 --> 00:06:58.124
- Who's stopping you, do you think?

00:06:58.124 --> 00:07:00.323
- Nobody, but you know,
look when I said I wanted

00:07:00.323 --> 00:07:02.453
to be an actor, people kind of chuckled,

00:07:02.453 --> 00:07:04.762
and then when I said I
wanted to be a serious actor,

00:07:04.762 --> 00:07:05.879
people chuckled.

00:07:05.879 --> 00:07:07.878
I could play the tough
guy role but when I wanted

00:07:07.878 --> 00:07:09.718
to do vulnerable thing in Boogie Nights,

00:07:09.718 --> 00:07:11.941
people were like, no, he's this other guy.

00:07:11.941 --> 00:07:14.517
And so, when I wanted to
do comedy, people were

00:07:14.517 --> 00:07:15.943
like, well you've been funny in movies,

00:07:15.943 --> 00:07:17.661
but comedy is a different thing.

00:07:17.661 --> 00:07:19.897
I didn't want to look like
an actor who could box,

00:07:19.897 --> 00:07:21.710
I wanted to look like a boxer who could

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:22.776
win the world title.

00:07:22.776 --> 00:07:24.127
I trained right here.

00:07:24.127 --> 00:07:25.811
- [Interviewer] At his
home in Beverly Hills.

00:07:25.811 --> 00:07:27.936
- This is where we
choreographed all the fights.

00:07:27.936 --> 00:07:30.556
- [Interviewer] Wahlberg built
a boxer mecca, complete with

00:07:30.556 --> 00:07:32.409
a top of the line ring.

00:07:32.409 --> 00:07:33.998
You took this seriously.

00:07:33.998 --> 00:07:36.948
- We were in here, eight, 10 hours a day.

00:07:36.948 --> 00:07:39.019
- [Interviewer] It was here
that he brought the real

00:07:39.019 --> 00:07:43.936
brothers, Micky and Dicky
to help with his training.

00:07:43.936 --> 00:07:46.958
Mark trained for the
role for four years, not

00:07:46.958 --> 00:07:48.978
knowing whether one frame of the film

00:07:48.978 --> 00:07:50.390
would ever be shot.

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:52.814
The Fighter almost didn't get made.

00:07:52.814 --> 00:07:55.727
Directors and co-stars came and went.

00:07:55.727 --> 00:07:58.133
Did you ever think, you
know, gosh, I'm never

00:07:58.133 --> 00:07:59.009
going to get this made.

00:07:59.009 --> 00:08:00.385
This is just impossible.

00:08:00.385 --> 00:08:03.243
- There were certainly
times where I would wake

00:08:03.243 --> 00:08:05.980
up at 4:30 in the morning,
my trainer would ring

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:07.781
the bell, and I'd be like, oh god.

00:08:07.781 --> 00:08:10.218
I'd be like, I better get this movie made.

00:08:10.218 --> 00:08:12.638
I'm going to kill somebody
if I don't get it made.

00:08:12.638 --> 00:08:13.998
- Did you get obsessed with it?

00:08:13.998 --> 00:08:16.121
- I was, yeah. I was.

00:08:16.121 --> 00:08:18.054
- What made you say this is the one?

00:08:18.054 --> 00:08:19.141
What was it about it?

00:08:19.141 --> 00:08:20.781
- I was such a huge Micky Ward fan.

00:08:20.781 --> 00:08:21.929
- Always, even growing up?

00:08:21.929 --> 00:08:24.428
- Yeah. Oh yeah, I mean,
god the name Micky Ward

00:08:24.428 --> 00:08:26.597
was to me, like the
name Larry Bird, just a

00:08:26.597 --> 00:08:28.097
local sports hero.

00:08:31.284 --> 00:08:32.882
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:08:32.882 --> 00:08:34.655
I made this video because Jay Starkilla

00:08:34.655 --> 00:08:36.216
asked me to, so if there's a famous

00:08:36.216 --> 00:08:38.428
entrepreneur that you
want me to profile next,

00:08:38.428 --> 00:08:39.740
leave it in the comments
below and I'll see

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:40.788
what I can do.

00:08:40.788 --> 00:08:42.926
I'd also love to know
which of Mark's 10 rules

00:08:42.926 --> 00:08:44.620
had the biggest impact on you and why,

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:46.807
what change you're going
to make in your life or

00:08:46.807 --> 00:08:48.307
business after watching this video,

00:08:48.307 --> 00:08:52.813
what had the richest,
thought process addition

00:08:52.813 --> 00:08:54.996
to you, leave it in the comments below

00:08:54.996 --> 00:08:56.508
and I will join in the discussion.

00:08:56.508 --> 00:08:58.065
Finally, I want to give
a quick shout out to

00:08:58.065 --> 00:09:00.311
Saif Murtaza, thank you
so much for buying a

00:09:00.311 --> 00:09:01.955
copy of my book, it
really means a lot to me.

00:09:01.955 --> 00:09:03.701
For those of you watching,
if you want a chance

00:09:03.701 --> 00:09:05.358
at a shout-out in the
future video, make sure

00:09:05.358 --> 00:09:07.100
to pick up a copy of the book and email in

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:09.566
your receipt so we can
keep track and give you

00:09:09.566 --> 00:09:10.705
the bonuses too.

00:09:10.705 --> 00:09:11.988
Thank you so much for watching.

00:09:11.988 --> 00:09:14.651
Continue to believe or
whatever your one word is,

00:09:14.651 --> 00:09:16.484
and I'll see you soon.

00:09:22.486 --> 00:09:25.799
- What one quality do
you think you've had that

00:09:25.799 --> 00:09:30.099
gave you, allowed you,
pushed you to achieve

00:09:30.099 --> 00:09:31.816
what you've had?

00:09:31.816 --> 00:09:34.826
- Um, I think my willingness to learn.

00:09:34.826 --> 00:09:37.899
I'm pretty relentless
when it comes to that.

00:09:37.899 --> 00:09:40.997
I certainly knew
everything about growing up

00:09:40.997 --> 00:09:43.216
in the street and what
it took to survive on

00:09:43.216 --> 00:09:46.108
the street corner of
Dorchester, but this is

00:09:46.108 --> 00:09:49.352
a whole new world to me, so you know, just

00:09:49.352 --> 00:09:51.292
being able to take advantage of the things

00:09:51.292 --> 00:09:54.545
I've seen, the things I've been exposed to

00:09:54.545 --> 00:09:56.661
now with my career, it's really been.

00:09:56.661 --> 00:09:58.075
- Now, you've been in
show business a long time,

00:09:58.075 --> 00:09:59.434
haven't you?
- Too long.

00:09:59.434 --> 00:10:00.874
- No, not too long.

00:10:00.874 --> 00:10:01.927
- It's starting to catch up to me.

00:10:01.927 --> 00:10:03.525
- How old were you when you first,

00:10:03.525 --> 00:10:04.854
when you were Marky Mark?

00:10:04.854 --> 00:10:06.766
- 18.
- 18 years old?

00:10:06.766 --> 00:10:08.478
- 18, yeah.

00:10:08.478 --> 00:10:11.478
(audience cheering)

00:10:14.435 --> 00:10:16.987
- I get all of the groups confused, it was

00:10:16.987 --> 00:10:18.383
New Kids in the Hall,

00:10:18.383 --> 00:10:20.616
- New Kids on the Block.
- New Kids on the Block!

00:10:20.616 --> 00:10:22.175
- Yeah. That was my brother.

00:10:22.175 --> 00:10:23.663
- That was your brother.
- Yeah.

00:10:23.663 --> 00:10:25.011
- Who's group were you in?

00:10:25.011 --> 00:10:28.028
- I was in Marky Mark and the Funky Bunch.

00:10:28.028 --> 00:10:32.023
I had been kind of of involved
with the New Kids before

00:10:32.023 --> 00:10:33.732
they actually formulated the group.

00:10:33.732 --> 00:10:35.255
My brother and I were
working with a producer,

00:10:35.255 --> 00:10:37.148
Maurice Starr, and then
we started out making

00:10:37.148 --> 00:10:39.756
a rap album and then
it turned into us going

00:10:39.756 --> 00:10:41.692
to vocal lessons and dance classes.

00:10:41.692 --> 00:10:43.154
I was like this is not what I want to do.

00:10:43.154 --> 00:10:44.946
Then I end up going to jail,
I'm watching them on TV.

00:10:44.946 --> 00:10:46.423
They're making millions
and millions of dollars.

00:10:46.423 --> 00:10:47.281
- Now wait a minute.

00:10:47.281 --> 00:10:48.639
- They performed at the jail all the time.

00:10:48.639 --> 00:10:50.521
- Now stop right there.

00:10:50.521 --> 00:10:52.905
How old were you when you went to jail?

00:10:52.905 --> 00:10:55.798
- 17.
- 17, and may I ask why?

00:10:55.798 --> 00:10:57.234
- For doing bad things.

00:10:57.234 --> 00:10:58.456
- Bad things.

00:10:58.456 --> 00:11:00.259
- And I don't recommend you
ever go there, especially

00:11:00.259 --> 00:11:01.781
with the calf situation.

00:11:01.781 --> 00:11:03.983
(audience laughter)

00:11:03.983 --> 00:11:06.150
Could be very problematic.

00:11:07.633 --> 00:11:09.893
- Again, another helpful piece of advice.

00:11:09.893 --> 00:11:13.568
Did it have to do something
with controlled substances?

00:11:13.568 --> 00:11:16.235
- That always weighs into the equation.

00:11:16.235 --> 00:11:17.735
- Was it thievery?

00:11:19.227 --> 00:11:21.139
- All of those things, all of the above.

00:11:21.139 --> 00:11:22.313
- How long were you gone?

00:11:22.313 --> 00:11:24.183
- Long enough to never want to go back.

00:11:24.183 --> 00:11:26.225
All together, I did like
two and a half months,

00:11:26.225 --> 00:11:29.219
but being tried as an adult at 17.

00:11:29.219 --> 00:11:31.368
I was probably 5'1", 110 pounds.

00:11:31.368 --> 00:11:34.049
- So, your brother and
his buddies are performing

00:11:34.049 --> 00:11:35.959
on TV, you're in prison.

00:11:35.959 --> 00:11:38.102
- Yeah, and then they came
to the jail to perform,

00:11:38.102 --> 00:11:40.557
and then I'd be watching
in the rec room, I'd

00:11:40.557 --> 00:11:41.749
be like, that's my brother.

00:11:41.749 --> 00:11:43.048
They'd be like, shut up.

00:11:43.048 --> 00:11:44.920
No, I was in that group.

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:46.538
They'd be like, well
you're the dumbest (beep)

00:11:46.538 --> 00:11:48.384
ever, excuse my language.

00:11:48.384 --> 00:11:50.634
because now you're in here.

00:11:54.131 --> 00:11:57.805
But I was lucky enough
that my brother came

00:11:57.805 --> 00:12:00.303
back and you know, when
I got out, he produced

00:12:00.303 --> 00:12:04.224
my album and then I was
able to start my own career.

00:12:04.224 --> 00:12:05.724
- Words of wisdom.

00:12:06.569 --> 00:12:08.955
Well, by god that that's
it for heavens' sakes.

00:12:08.955 --> 00:12:10.303
And now look at you,

00:12:10.303 --> 00:12:12.089
you're a big time movie star,

00:12:12.089 --> 00:12:13.498
you've got a lovely family,

00:12:13.498 --> 00:12:17.278
you've learned a lessons
the hard way in life.

00:12:17.278 --> 00:12:19.409
Now, do you're kids know
about your time away?

00:12:19.409 --> 00:12:21.158
- No, they don't.

00:12:21.158 --> 00:12:22.394
- Will that be a difficult...

00:12:22.394 --> 00:12:24.307
That won't be a difficult
conversation, will it?

00:12:24.307 --> 00:12:25.947
- No more difficult
than daddy didn't finish

00:12:25.947 --> 00:12:28.122
high school, although I am going back now,

00:12:28.122 --> 00:12:29.994
they have this new
program in Massachusetts

00:12:29.994 --> 00:12:32.278
where you can actually
take the courses online,

00:12:32.278 --> 00:12:34.424
so I'm starting this month in New Orleans,

00:12:34.424 --> 00:12:36.994
and I'm going to go and
get my high school diploma.

00:12:36.994 --> 00:12:40.584
(audience cheers)

00:12:40.584 --> 00:12:42.790
Also, I'm the ambassador of Taco Bell's

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:45.111
Graduate to Go program, and
they asked me to do this,

00:12:45.111 --> 00:12:46.685
and I was like, dude,
I never graduated, why

00:12:46.685 --> 00:12:48.141
would you pick me to do this?

00:12:48.141 --> 00:12:50.491
- Is what you're doing, is
it different than the GED?

00:12:50.491 --> 00:12:52.769
- Yes, it's an actual
diploma, I've got to take

00:12:52.769 --> 00:12:54.691
up all the courses that I missed.

00:12:54.691 --> 00:12:57.651
I'm a little nervous, but
I've got a lot of time

00:12:57.651 --> 00:12:58.946
on my hands to study.

00:12:58.946 --> 00:13:02.622
- How much did you have to make up?

00:13:02.622 --> 00:13:03.961
- I never graduated the ninth grade.

00:13:03.961 --> 00:13:05.527
I quit during the ninth grade,

00:13:05.527 --> 00:13:08.570
so she said my geometry
scores are pretty good.

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:09.834
They're going to give me whatever credits

00:13:09.834 --> 00:13:10.971
I already have, which is probably like one

00:13:10.971 --> 00:13:13.834
or two, but I'm going to make it happen.

00:13:13.834 --> 00:13:15.269
It's one of those things, where I said,

00:13:15.269 --> 00:13:16.743
you know what, I'm
going to make it happen.

00:13:16.743 --> 00:13:18.604
- Now will it take another
three years do you think?

00:13:18.604 --> 00:13:19.779
- No, I could do it ...

00:13:19.779 --> 00:13:21.767
I have a lot of down time
on sets in the trailer

00:13:21.767 --> 00:13:23.961
and stuff, so I just
want to blast through it

00:13:23.961 --> 00:13:25.214
and hopefully be able to do it in maybe

00:13:25.214 --> 00:13:26.172
six or eight months.

00:13:26.172 --> 00:13:27.452
- Maybe go out for the track team?

00:13:27.452 --> 00:13:28.607
- I would love to.

00:13:28.607 --> 00:13:30.433
I would love to.

00:13:30.433 --> 00:13:32.284
I want the whole deal.

00:13:32.284 --> 00:13:35.578
I want the cap and gown
and it's important,

00:13:35.578 --> 00:13:36.996
no matter how old you are.

00:13:36.996 --> 00:13:40.829
I'd love to, at some
point, go to film school.

00:13:42.981 --> 00:13:45.148
- This is a great example, for not just

00:13:45.148 --> 00:13:48.853
your family, but I mean,
my god, because how

00:13:48.853 --> 00:13:51.415
many people are in a situation that seems

00:13:51.415 --> 00:13:52.649
insurmountable.

00:13:52.649 --> 00:13:55.205
At 17, who would've bet on you?

00:13:55.205 --> 00:13:57.072
Honest to god, who would've?

00:13:57.072 --> 00:13:58.228
And now look at you.

00:13:58.228 --> 00:13:59.527
- I'm very fortunate.

00:13:59.527 --> 00:14:02.114
- And why, what is different about you?

00:14:02.114 --> 00:14:04.300
What changed in your life, was it

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:05.876
the stretch in prison?

00:14:05.876 --> 00:14:06.709
Something else?

00:14:06.709 --> 00:14:08.127
- A lot of things, you know, I'm very much

00:14:08.127 --> 00:14:10.707
into my faith and I just wanted to turn my

00:14:10.707 --> 00:14:11.942
life around.

00:14:11.942 --> 00:14:13.768
No matter what I was doing,
whether I was working

00:14:13.768 --> 00:14:15.871
on a construction site,
driving a tow truck,

00:14:15.871 --> 00:14:17.811
which was my last real
job, whatever I was doing,

00:14:17.811 --> 00:14:19.500
I was going to end up on
the right side of the law.

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:23.221
I was going to be a positive
and productive person.

